<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Marathon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/490-bce">490 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Greco-Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Marathon was fought in 490 BCE on the plain of Marathon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, where an Athenian army, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a>, defeated a Persian expeditionary force sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>. It ended the first Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-persian-invasion-of-greece">invasion of Greece</a>. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> the setback was limited in scale, but the battle became a central symbol of Greek resistance to Persian expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-marathon.jpg" alt="Battle of Marathon" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Steeple Davis · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Scene_of_the_Battle_of_Marathon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and Eretria had each sent a force to support the Ionian cities in their revolt against Persian rule, and the Athenians and Eretrians captured and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardis">Sardis</a> before retreating with heavy losses. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> swore to burn down the two cities. After the Persian victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lade">Battle of Lade</a> in 494 BCE crushed the revolt, he began planning to subjugate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In 490 BCE he sent a naval force under Datis and Artaphernes across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>, which captured Eretria and then landed at the bay of Marathon, about 27 kilometres north-east of Athens.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, the Athenian general with most experience of fighting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, the Athenian army marched to block the two exits from the plain. The Spartans, in the middle of a religious festival, said they could not march until the full moon, but 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plataea">Plataea</a> joined the Athenians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Ancient sources give the Athenians and Plataeans as about 10,000 men, a figure modern historians generally accept. Modern estimates for the Persian infantry run from 20,000 to 100,000, with a consensus of perhaps 25,000. The Athenians, probably outnumbered at least two to one, had no need to seek battle, since each day brought the Spartans closer, and the marshes and mountains kept the Persian cavalry from joining the infantry.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> ordered a general attack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> thinned their centre, perhaps so that their line was as long as the Persian line, and ran for the last stretch under the Persian arrows. The Persian centre prevailed against the thin Greek centre, but on both wings the Athenians and Plataeans routed the Persian levies and turned inwards to surround the Persian centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> broke in panic towards their ships, and some ran into the swamps, where an unknown number drowned. The Athenians captured seven ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> records 6,400 Persian bodies counted on the battlefield and 192 Athenian dead, among them the war archon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>. The Persian fleet then sailed towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, but the Athenian army marched back quickly and arrived in time to prevent a landing, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. The Spartan army reached Marathon the next day and agreed that the Athenians had won a great victory. Darius's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes I</a> began the second invasion in 480 BCE.</p><p>The defeat barely touched the resources of the Persian Empire, yet it was the first Greek victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> and showed that resistance was possible. It also showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> could win without the Spartans, and it raised Greek esteem for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Marathon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Greek Wars showed the limits of Persian arms against determined Greek hoplites and fleets, at <b>Marathon</b> (490 BCE), Salamis (480 BCE) and Plataea (479 BCE).</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 490 BCE, under the generalship of Miltiades, about 10,000 Athenians and Plataeans defeated the Persian landing force at the <b>Battle of Marathon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Herodotus records that Persians and Saka broke the Athenian centre at the <b>Battle of Marathon</b> in 490 BCE and that Saka horsemen fought at Plataea in 479 BCE, and Saka cavalry served Darius III at the Battle of Gaugamela in 331 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the century after Cleisthenes its citizens beat the Persians at the <b>Battle of Marathon</b> in 490 BCE, helped to defeat them at the Battle of Salamis in 480 BCE, built the Parthenon and staged the dramas of Aeschylus, Sophocles and Euripides.</blockquote><a href="/government/direct-democracy">Direct democracy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>…earlier sanctuary for Athena Parthenos, the Older Parthenon, had been begun on the same site shortly after the <b>Battle of Marathon</b>, on a limestone foundation that extended the southern part of the Acropolis summit; it was still under construction when the Persians sacked the city in 480 BCE…</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They provided the hoplites, the heavy infantry who paid for their own shield, helmet, spear and armour and fought shoulder to shoulder in the phalanx, as at the <b>Battle of Marathon</b> in 490 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li></ul></section>
